WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.429
Welcome to Module Eight: Run, Monitor, and Maintain.

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.290
Shipping an agent is where the real job starts.

3
00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.108
Picture QuickBite,

4
00:00:08.158 --> 00:00:12.437
a food delivery app with an AI support agent for late orders and refunds.

5
00:00:12.767 --> 00:00:15.128
At launch it works well, and the team moves on.

6
00:00:15.928 --> 00:00:19.611
For a month, nobody deploys anything, and the logs show no errors.

7
00:00:19.941 --> 00:00:21.513
Every dashboard stays green.

8
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:25.932
Yet refunds creep up, and steps per chat climb from four to eight.

9
00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:28.452
The cost of each resolved ticket doubles.

10
00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:31.890
Nobody notices until customers complain.

11
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.711
Their monitoring could say a chat took nine seconds, but never why.

12
00:00:36.511 --> 00:00:41.133
So they add agent traces, where every model call and tool call becomes a span.

13
00:00:41.463 --> 00:00:46.619
Each span records the prompt version, tokens, cost, and the tool's exact arguments.

14
00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:49.182
Within minutes, the pattern appears.

15
00:00:49.512 --> 00:00:52.726
A partner's order-status tool had changed its format,

16
00:00:52.776 --> 00:00:55.435
so the agent retried, gave up, and refunded.

17
00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:58.657
They switch to a safe fallback, fix the tool,

18
00:00:58.707 --> 00:01:00.964
and add the case to their golden test set.

19
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:04.679
A scheduled contract test now checks that tool every day.

20
00:01:05.479 --> 00:01:08.885
Lesson one is observability: what every span must carry.

21
00:01:09.215 --> 00:01:14.199
OpenTelemetry lets you instrument once and send the same traces to any backend.

22
00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:17.094
You'll learn which signals to alert on,

23
00:01:17.144 --> 00:01:19.899
like task success, tool errors, and steps per task.

24
00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:23.144
The one that matters most is cost per resolved task.

25
00:01:23.944 --> 00:01:28.011
You'll also capture feedback, from thumbs to quiet signs like rephrasing.

26
00:01:28.341 --> 00:01:30.894
Tied to traces, it feeds a data flywheel.

27
00:01:31.694 --> 00:01:35.889
Lesson two covers four kinds of drift: input, corpus, model, and tool.

28
00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:38.836
Agents degrade even when nobody touches them.

29
00:01:39.636 --> 00:01:41.448
The fixes: pin model versions,

30
00:01:41.498 --> 00:01:46.413
run a golden set against production on a schedule, and contract-test every tool.

31
00:01:46.743 --> 00:01:48.826
Track each metric against a baseline.

32
00:01:49.626 --> 00:01:52.719
When an incident hits: contain, diagnose from traces,

33
00:01:52.769 --> 00:01:55.507
fix the right layer, and add a regression test.

34
00:01:55.837 --> 00:01:57.749
Then ask what guard would have caught it sooner.

35
00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:01.867
Prompts, schemas, and model versions are versioned together,

36
00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:04.729
then ship through shadow, canary, and full rollout.

37
00:02:05.059 --> 00:02:08.017
Even a one-word prompt edit is a production change.

38
00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:11.248
This domain is five percent of the exam,

39
00:02:11.298 --> 00:02:15.466
and a classic question is an agent that degrades with no deployment:

40
00:02:15.516 --> 00:02:17.513
the answer is an upstream change.

41
00:02:17.843 --> 00:02:20.929
Let's start with lesson one, observability for agents.
